{
  "image_name": "77a5a502f80f62590d369855ec21ca72.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a conventional CO2 reduction cell with a modified design from Wang and colleagues, highlighting how the newer setup appears to enable direct formic acid production.\n\nIn the conventional cell, CO2 enters at the cathode and is reduced to formate (HCOO−) in a liquid bicarbonate electrolyte containing K+ and HCO3−. A cation-exchange membrane separates the cathode and anode compartments, and H+ moves across this membrane. On the anode side, water is oxidized to O2. The overall picture suggests that the product remains as dissolved formate in a bulk aqueous electrolyte rather than being recovered directly as formic acid.\n\nIn the Wang design, the central region is a solid electrolyte instead of a liquid electrolyte. Humidified CO2 is fed to the cathode side, where formate is generated, while H+ comes from the anode side across a membrane. Inside the solid electrolyte, HCOO− and H+ combine to form HCOOH, shown as the collected product leaving downward from the middle. Water and oxygen are still associated with the anode side. Overall, the main contrast is the shift from producing formate in solution to coupling ions within a solid electrolyte to obtain formic acid directly.",
